b. Fishing license and other requirements. The requisites set out by the law for engaging in industrial or commercial fishing, whether for shrimp or other marine species, are contained in Decree-law 17 of 1959, and especially in Decree 42 of 1965 and decrees amending it, and more recently in Cabinet Decree 15 of January 27, 1972 which provides for a tax on sport and recreational fishing and navigation (see the Chapter on Taxation).
Decree-law 17 of 1959 (amended by article 11, paragraph 1 of Cabinet Decree 225 of 1969), provides that the fishing license is obtained from the Department of Fisheries and Related Industries of the Ministry of Commerce and Industries. Collective fishing licenses are available and are issued when the members of a crew are concerned, but when one of such persons wishes to engage individually in fishing, he must obtain an individual license. Fishing licenses are nontransferrable.
Possession of a fishing license exempts the shipowner from securing a commercial permit with respect to fishing vessels, but does not exempt the owner who engages on land in trade, warehousing or processing of such products.
Decree 42 of January 24, 1965 provides that all natural or juridical persons who wish to engage in commercial or industrial fishing in Panamanian territorial waters using vessels greater than 20 gross tons must secure a fishing license for each vessel operated, to be issued by the Ministry of Commerce and Industries through the Department of Fisheries and Related Industries.
Shrimping vessels of less than 20 gross tons may not be replaced by others of tonnage greater than that specified herein.
Fishing licenses are issued in the name of the vessel, expire on December 31 of each year, and must be renewed prior to that date.
The request for each license and its later renewal is made in the following manner:
 In duplicate, on a form provided by the Department of Fisheries and Related Industries. Each original must have affixed to it fiscal stamps of a value of B/2.00 and a "soldier" stamp.
 The owner of the vessel must secure a paid-up tax certificate.
 In addition, the owner of the vessel must submit to the Department of Fisheries and Related Industries a temporary or permanent navigation permit, the latter duly recorded in the Registry of Property. This also applies in those cases in which the request is made for the first time, or after a sale or transfer of the vessel.
